commons shop.values

---
A product's stock-keeping unit. Opaque, so a sku can't be passed where an
order id is expected, or the other way round, although both are strings.
---
type Sku = opaque String where NonEmpty

---
The id the customer chooses for an order, from the URL. Opaque for the same
reason as `Sku`.
---
type OrderId = opaque String where NonEmpty

---
How many units of one sku an order or a restock moves. Zero and negative
quantities are refused at the boundary, before any handler runs.
---
type Quantity = Int where InRange(1, 1000)

---
A price in cents. At least one cent, and at most 100,000.00.
---
type Cents = Int where InRange(1, 10000000)

---
The order id as plain text, for the payment processor, which knows nothing of
the shop's types, and as a map key (see `skuRef`). Only this commons can read
an `OrderId`'s string.
---
fn orderRef(id: OrderId) -> String { id.raw }

---
The sku as plain text, for a map key. A `Map` keyed by an opaque type compiles
to TypeScript that `tsc` rejects when its keys are collected
(accuser/bynk#1982), so the shop keys its maps by text and keeps the opaque
value inside the entry.
---
fn skuRef(s: Sku) -> String { s.raw }

---
Non-empty text: what a `Sku` or an `OrderId` is made of.
---
type Label = String where NonEmpty

---
A sku from text already known to be non-empty, so no `Result` is needed. A
literal is checked when it is compiled: `skuFrom("")` is refused. The commons
may use `.unsafe` here because `Label` has already established the predicate.
---
fn skuFrom(text: Label) -> Sku { Sku.unsafe(text) }

---
An order id from non-empty text, as `skuFrom`.
---
fn orderIdFrom(text: Label) -> OrderId { OrderId.unsafe(text) }
